<h2>Start with registrations, not price</h2>
<p>The first thing to check on any Indian exporter isn't their price — it's whether they're a real, registered business. Two registrations matter:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>IEC (Import Export Code).</strong> Issued by India's DGFT, this is the base legal requirement to export from India. No legitimate exporter operates without one. Ask for it directly, and if a supplier is cagey about sharing it, that alone is worth pausing on.</li>
  <li><strong>APEDA registration.</strong> For agricultural and processed food exporters specifically. If you're buying fresh produce, grains, spices, or similar categories, this is the second thing to ask about.</li>
</ul>
<p>See our own <a href="/registrations/">Registrations page</a> for an example of how a legitimate exporter should present this information — plainly, with actual numbers, not vague assurances.</p>

<h2>Ask for references, and actually check them</h2>
<p>A supplier with real buyer relationships should be willing to connect you with at least one existing customer, or provide enough detail (destination markets, approximate volumes, how long they've worked together) that you can sanity-check the claim. This isn't foolproof — references can be cherry-picked — but a total unwillingness to provide any track record is itself informative.</p>

<h2>Start smaller than you're comfortable with</h2>
<p>This is the single most effective risk-reduction tool available to you, and it's underused. A trial order — smaller than your eventual target volume — lets you evaluate whether a supplier actually delivers what they specify, on the timeline they promise, with proper documentation, before you commit real money to a full container or a recurring contract.</p>
<p>If a supplier resists a trial order and pushes hard for a large first commitment, ask why. Sometimes there's a legitimate reason (minimum processing runs, a specific harvest window). Often it's simply that a trial order doesn't suit their cash-flow needs — which tells you something about their financial position, not yours.</p>

<h2>Structure payment to protect yourself</h2>
<p>For a first order of meaningful size, a Letter of Credit is the standard tool for a reason: your bank releases payment only once the exporter presents documents proving the goods were shipped as agreed. This doesn't guarantee the goods match the contract in every respect, but it does mean you're not simply wiring money on trust.</p>
<p>For smaller trial orders, a partial advance with the balance due against shipping documents is common and reasonable. What should make you uncomfortable is a request for 100% advance payment on a large, first-time order from an unverified supplier — that shifts essentially all the risk onto you.</p>
<p>See our <a href="/payment-terms/">Payment Terms</a> page for more detail on how these structures work.</p>

<h2>Watch how they handle specification questions</h2>
<p>A legitimate, experienced exporter should be comfortable discussing specification in precise, measurable terms — moisture percentage, grade, size, packaging format, whatever applies to the product. If every answer is vague ("premium quality," "export grade," with no numbers attached), that's worth noting. Specific businesses give specific answers; the ones who don't may not have the operational discipline to actually deliver against a contract.</p>

<h2>Red flags, summarized</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Unwillingness to share IEC or APEDA registration details</li>
  <li>No references, or refusal to discuss existing buyer relationships even in general terms</li>
  <li>Pressure for full advance payment on a first, large order</li>
  <li>Vague, non-numeric answers to specification questions</li>
  <li>Prices significantly below every other quote you've received, with no clear explanation</li>
</ul>
<p>None of these alone is disqualifying — but two or three together are a strong signal to slow down, verify independently, and start smaller than originally planned.</p>
